WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:05.500
Two state laws shape how every local body does its work in public.

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:06.246
The Ralph M.

3
00:00:06.246 --> 00:00:07.923
Brown Act governs meetings.

4
00:00:07.923 --> 00:00:11.154
The California Public Records Act governs documents.

5
00:00:11.154 --> 00:00:17.180
Both changed recently, and both catch experienced officials who assume the old rules still apply.

6
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.369
Start with what counts as a meeting.

7
00:00:19.369 --> 00:00:28.734
Under the Brown Act, a meeting is any gathering of a majority of a body to hear, discuss, deliberate, or take action on something within its jurisdiction.

8
00:00:28.734 --> 00:00:30.011
Notice the word hear.

9
00:00:30.011 --> 00:00:36.275
A majority sitting together at a developer's presentation can be a meeting, even if nobody says a word.

10
00:00:36.275 --> 00:00:38.585
The bigger trap is the serial meeting.

11
00:00:38.585 --> 00:00:46.248
A majority may not use a series of communications, directly or through intermediaries, to deliberate outside a public meeting.

12
00:00:46.248 --> 00:00:50.626
That covers a chain, where one member tells a second, who tells a third.

13
00:00:50.626 --> 00:00:55.673
It also covers a hub, where a staff member or a lobbyist polls each member in turn.

14
00:00:55.673 --> 00:01:01.329
Staff may brief members one at a time, but they may not tell you where your colleagues stand.

15
00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:07.957
If a manager ever says three of your colleagues are fine with this, the briefing has just become a violation.

16
00:01:07.957 --> 00:01:11.241
Social media has its own rule, made permanent in 2026.

17
00:01:11.241 --> 00:01:15.498
You may post, answer questions, and ask for input on public platforms.

18
00:01:15.498 --> 00:01:23.160
But you may not respond to a colleague's post about a matter in your jurisdiction, and responding includes a like or an emoji.

19
00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:29.565
Agendas must be posted seventy-two hours before a regular meeting and twenty-four hours before a special one.

20
00:01:29.565 --> 00:01:41.553
Items not on the agenda can be acted on only in an emergency, when a need arose after posting and two thirds of the body agree, or when an item was continued from a meeting no more than five days earlier.

21
00:01:41.553 --> 00:01:47.018
Otherwise you can respond briefly, ask a question, or ask staff to put it on a future agenda.

22
00:01:47.018 --> 00:01:57.772
Closed session is limited to a short list: real property negotiations, litigation, personnel matters, labor negotiations with a negotiator named in public first, and security threats.

23
00:01:57.772 --> 00:02:04.060
Final actions and votes are reported out afterward, and leaking what was said inside is itself a violation.

24
00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:06.778
Remote attendance now runs on several tracks.

25
00:02:06.778 --> 00:02:14.446
The traditional rule lets a member join from anywhere, as long as that location is listed on the agenda and open to the public.

26
00:02:14.446 --> 00:02:26.705
A newer just cause rule lets a member join from an unlisted location for reasons like childcare, illness, or official travel, as long as a quorum is together in person and the remote member is on camera.

27
00:02:26.705 --> 00:02:30.630
Those just cause appearances are capped at a few meetings a year.

28
00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:47.960
Beginning July first, 2026, councils in cities the size of Torrance carry new duties: two-way remote public comment, a plan for when the connection fails, a meetings web page, and translated agendas for any language spoken by twenty percent or more of residents who have limited English.

29
00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:48.891
Now the records.

30
00:02:48.891 --> 00:02:56.335
The agency has ten days to decide whether records can be released, with one extension of up to fourteen days for listed reasons.

31
00:02:56.335 --> 00:03:07.328
And here is the rule officials most often forget: under a 2017 California Supreme Court decision, messages about public business on your personal phone or personal email are public records.

32
00:03:07.328 --> 00:03:11.806
The city can ask you to search your own accounts and swear to what you found.

33
00:03:11.806 --> 00:03:14.423
Never delete a text thread with an applicant.

34
00:03:14.423 --> 00:03:18.960
For more, see the legal ground rules section of the Local Official's Handbook.
